  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**:\n   - **Central Idea**: Transform the step function representation into a symmetric, piecewise-constant function optimized using discrete Fourier transform (DFT) and gradient descent methods to minimize maximum overlap.\n   - **Why Different**: Instead of direct optimization in high-dimensional space, we enforce symmetry to reduce dimensionality, use DFT to efficiently compute overlaps, and employ projected gradient descent with adaptive step sizes.\n   - **Key Bottleneck**: High dimensionality (95 points) makes direct optimization prohibitive. Symmetry assumption reduces degrees of freedom, and DFTs speed up overlap calculations.\n   - **Limitations**: Assumes symmetry; may miss asymmetric optimums. Active-set handling might need extension for arbitrary peaks.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**:\n   - **Variables**: \\( h = (h_0, h_1, \\dots, h_{127}) \\) (if n_points=128) or adjusted via integer interpolation from parent at 95 points.\n   - **Objective**: Minimize \\( C_5 = \\max_k \\sum_{i} h_i (1-h_{i+k}) \\, dx \\) scaled by dx.\n   - **Constraints**: \n     - \\( 0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\)\n     - \\( \\sum h_i \\cdot dx = 1 \\)\n   - **Representation**: Piecewise-constant with peaks at 0.5*(2*j + 1) for j=0..m-1 (symmetric around center).\n   - **Gradient Handling**: \n     \\(\\nabla h_k = dx \\cdot \\sum_{j: j+k \\in \\text{active}} (1 - h_{j+k})\\), considering alignment indices.\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**:\n   - **Input & Initialization**:\n     - Convert parent (95 samples) to 128-point symmetric representation via interpolation.\n     - Let \\( h \\) be a vector of length n_points (default 128) with \\(n = 2*m\\) samples.\n   - **Objective Function**:\n     - Compute DFT for fixed integral regions.\n     - \\(\\text{Overlap}(k) = dx \\cdot |\\text{DFT}(h) \\cdot \\text{DFT}(1-h)|\\) magnitudes, but note linear convolution.\n     - Actually: Use numpy.correlate in 'full' mode scaled by dx.\n   - **Gradient Calculation**:\n     - Stencil-based for gradient \\( \\nabla (h)_{i} \\approx dx \\cdot \\sum_{j} h_j \\cdot (1-h_{|k|+j}) \\) for shifted indices.\n   - **Optimization Loop**:\n     - **Projected Gradient Descent**:\n       - While stopping condition:\n         - Compute gradient \\( g = \\nabla C_5 \\)\n         - Update \\( h \\leftarrow h - \\alpha g \\)\n         - Project to bounds: \\( h \\leftarrow \\max(0, \\min(1, h)) \\)\n         - Adjust integral: Scale \\( h \\leftarrow h \\cdot \\frac{47.5}{\\text{sum}(h)} \\) (if sum(h) \\(\\neq\\) 47.5)\n   - **NHitsEdge Handling**:\n     - If \\( h_i \\) nears 0 or 1, use Melaset technique: keep only peak rows and reduce dimension.\n   - **Multiple Peaks**: Alternate between unit interval shifts and large interval optimization.\n\n4. **Search Choices**:\n   - **DHits Intervals**: Use 4 to 8 peaks periodically adapted via gradient severity.\n   - **Step Size**: Initialize \\( \\alpha=0.1 \\), halve if gradient not descending.\n   - **Batching**: Process in blocks, break on significant improvement.\n   - **Parthenogenetic Extension**: For local extrema, random mutations.\n\n5. **Budget and Robustness**:\n   - **Time Estimation**: \n     - DFT-based evaluations: \\(O(n \\log n)\\) per point.\n     - Gradient computation: O(n\u00b2) for full convolution, but optimized with numpy.correlate.\n     - **n\u2248128**:\n       - Best-case evaluations/s: ~50\n       - Total possible: ~22,000.\n   - **Fallback**:\n     - Save every top-10 improvement.\n     - On failure (numpy errors, gradient explosion): revert to parent.\n   - **Recovery**:\n     - Use exponential backoff for subdimensional projections.\n\n6. **Validation and Output**:\n   - **Feasibility Checks**:\n     - \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\)\n     - \\(|\\text{sum}(h) \\cdot dx - 1| \\leq 1e-5\\)\n     - Reproduce C5 calculation with tolerance 1e-4.\n   - **Short-Circuit**:\n     - Early return if better than current best.\n   - **Final Checks**: Manual difference-check for top-3 solutions.\n   - **NPoints Flexibility**:\n     - Start from interpolation of n=95 parent.\n     - Handle even/odd dimensions via nearest even m.",
